{
  "_help": "One battle. at is a place on the map as fractions of its width and height: [0, 0] is the north-west corner, [1, 1] the south-east. Teams with default true (the default) are the historical force; the rest can be chosen instead before the battle. budget is the points a side may spend; leave it out to use the cost of the historical force. men gives the soldiers' names; fewer names than the team has places makes a smaller team.",
  "id": "pothus",
  "order": 2,
  "title": "Pothus, 25 May 1940",
  "map": "pothus",
  "minutes": 60,
  "prepMinutes": 10,
  "briefing": "Two days later and 25 km further north. Gubbins needs time to get his brigade into a last line at Finneid, and has told Major Stockwell to hold Pothus, where the side valley joins Saltdalselva. The girder bridge was blown in the small hours, too early and only partly. No. 1 Company of the Irish Guards holds the steep ridge between the rivers in front of the bridge, from trenches; No. 4 Company covers the bridge from the west bank with No. 3 Company behind it. A Norwegian machine-gun company with two mortars stands on the height west of the road, and a troop of 25-pounders is firing over open sights. The Independent Companies are in reserve. German cyclists meet the outposts at eight in the morning. Hold the flags for an hour; the bridge and the ridge count double.",
  "alliedRole": "Defend. Irish Guards: two sections of No. 1 Company on the ridge and one at Pothus farm, two sections of No. 4 Company at the bridge, No. 3 Company behind them. Two Norwegian mortars and a Norwegian machine gun on the west bank, Independent Company men in reserve. Stockforce HQ can call the 25-pounders, which fire over open sights: quick and accurate.",
  "germanRole": "Attack. Two cycle squads lead, with six squads, the Zugtrupp and a mortar behind them. The Zugtrupp can call the mountain guns twice and a flight of Heinkels. Storming the ridge head-on cost the Germans dearly: work round its flanks. The river can be waded at two gravel bars upstream.",
